{"attack": true, "case": "The target's claim states that preference should go to 'the theory subjected to the most rigorous scrutiny, regardless of outcome.' Taken literally, this entails preferring a theory that was severely tested and FAILED over one that was lightly tested and survived—since the failed theory was subjected to more rigorous scrutiny and the claim explicitly disregards outcome. This is a reductio of the proposal: it dissolves the tension by destroying the distinction between corroborated and refuted theories. Compounding this, the mechanism section contradicts the claim by saying we focus on 'the theory that has survived the hardest tests'—introducing an outcome condition ('survived') that the claim explicitly set aside ('regardless of outcome'). The target is internally inconsistent and yields an absurd prescription.", "counterexample": ["Theory A is subjected to one trivial test and survives. Theory B is subjected to the most severe test in the field and fails it. Under the claim's rule ('most rigorous scrutiny, regardless of outcome'), we must prefer Theory B—the refuted one—over Theory A."], "premise": "The claim says preference is 'regardless of outcome,' so a theory that fails the hardest tests is preferred over one that passes easier tests; but the mechanism restricts attention to theories that 'survived' the hardest tests, reintroducing the very outcome-dependence the claim purports to eliminate.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "39d524daa346c261606aab3769d4c69c65f8df8e8b84c0c24be88a44273def5d", "quote": "the preference is then for the theory subjected to the most rigorous scrutiny, regardless of outcome, shifting the focus from the theory's reliability to the community's diligence."}, {"block": "39d524daa346c261606aab3769d4c69c65f8df8e8b84c0c24be88a44273def5d", "quote": "we focus our resources on the theory that has survived the hardest tests because it represents the current frontier of knowledge, not because it is 'safer' to use."}], "successor_question": "Can an account that selects theories by 'severity of tests attempted, regardless of outcome' avoid preferring refuted theories, without silently reintroducing an outcome condition?"}